  <content>
  <section><title>Working with Expressions of the Form \((px + m)(qx + n)\)</title>
<para>You have factored and solved many different types of <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic equations</term>, but the leading <term class="glossary-tooltip">coefficient</term> of the \(x^2\) term was often 1.</para>
<para>Let’s look at the <term class="glossary-tooltip">factored form</term> of a more complex expression to better understand the process.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>Write \((4x + 1)(x + 3)\) in <term class="glossary-tooltip">standard form</term>.</para>
<para>To find the <term class="glossary-tooltip">quadratic expression</term> in standard form, use the distributive property of multiplication over addition, or FOIL.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\left(4x+1\right)\left(x+3\right)&amp;=&amp; 4x^2+12x+x+3\\&amp;=&amp; 4x^2+13x+3\end{array}\)</para>
<para>Now, let’s try to go the other direction from standard form to factored form.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Write \(2x^2+ 13x + 15\) in factored form.</para>
<para>Since the leading coefficient is 2, we know the factored form must have the structure:</para>
<para>\((2x +\_\_\_)(x + \_\_\_)\)</para>
<para>Since the constant term is +15, the two missing numbers in the factored form must have a product of 15.</para>
<para>The possibilities include:</para>
<para> +1 and +15
  +3 and +5
  -1 and -15
  -3 and -5</para>
<para>Since the middle term, \(13x\), is positive, we know that only the positive factors need to be considered.</para>
<para>Since the two factors are already different inside the parentheses, the order in which we place the numbers is important. We will try both, if necessary.</para>
<para>Let’s try \({\style{color:red}1}\) and \({\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\).</para>
<para>\(\left(2x+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:red}1}\right)\)
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+2x+15x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+17x+15\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
<para>Try reordering the same numbers.</para>
<para>\(\left(2x+{\style{color:red}1}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}5}\right)\)\(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+30x+x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+31x+15\end{array}\) not equivalent to the original expression</para>
<para>Let’s try \({\style{color:blue}3}\) and \({\style{color:blue}5}\).</para>
<para>\(\left(2x+{\style{color:blue}5}\right)\left(x+{\style{color:blue}3}\right)\)
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+6x+5x+15\\&amp;=&amp;2x^2+11x+15\end{array}\)
  not equivalent to the original expression</para>
<para>Try reordering the same numbers.</para>
<para>\(\left(2x + {\style{color:blue}3}\right)\left(x + {\style{color:blue}5}\right)\)
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp;2x^2+ 10x + 3x + 15 \\
  &amp;=&amp;2x^2+ 13x + 15\end{array}\)
  \({\style{color:red}\checkmark}\) equivalent to the original expression</para>
<para>So, the factored form of \(2x^2 + 13x + 15\) is \((2x + 3)(x + 5)\).</para>
<note class="try"><title>Try It: Working with Expressions of the Form \((px + m)(qx + n)\)</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Find the factored form of \(3x^2− 11x − 4\).</para>
  
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to find the factored form:</para>
  
  <para>The factored form will have the structure: \((3x +\_\_\_)(x +\_\_\_)\).</para>
  
  <para>The factors of -4 are:</para>
  
  <para>-4 and 1
    4 and -1
    -2 and 2</para>
  
  <para>Try -4 and 1.</para>
  
  <para>\(\left(3x-4\right)\left(x+1\right)\)
    \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-4x+3x-4\\&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-x-4\end{array}\)
    not equivalent to the original expression</para>
  
  <para>Try reordering.</para>
  
  <para>\(\left(3x+1\right)\left(x-4\right)\)
    \(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-12x+x-4\\&amp;=&amp; 3x^2-11x-4\end{array}\)
    \({\style{color:red}\checkmark}\)equivalent to the original expression</para>
  
  <para>The factored form of \(3x^2− 11x − 4\) is \((3x + 1)(x − 4)\).</para>

</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
